<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Tuscia</h1><p class="st-meta">576 CE – 797 CE (approx.) · capital Lucca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lombard duchy of Tuscany, centred on Lucca, whose duke Desiderius rose to become the last king of the Lombards.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Lombards</a> occupied most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a> in the 570s, the region was organised under dukes, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a> as the chief seat. Smaller duchies existed at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/clusium">Chiusi</a> and elsewhere, but Lucca, on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-francigena">Via Francigena</a>, emerged as the dominant centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of the early dukes. Documents from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a>, unusually rich for the 8th century, show a society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> landowners, churches and notaries, and Duke Walpert appears in the early 8th century. The Lombard nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> held authority in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/march-of-tuscany">Tuscia</a> before becoming king in 756. Lucca minted gold coins and was the main town of Lombard <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne's</a> conquest of 774, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/march-of-tuscany">Tuscia</a> passed to Frankish counts and dukes based at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a>. By the 9th century these governors were styled margraves, and the region became the March of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-tuscia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Tuscia" width="320" height="265"><figcaption>Image: Wario2 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lombard_(Kingdom_of)_Italy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>576 CE – 797 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lucca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walpert, Desiderius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lombardic, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lombard Tuscia, Duchy of Lucca</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=686&amp;focus=duchy-of-tuscia" aria-label="Duchy of Tuscia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4932 624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Tuscia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5252 880L5218 839M5252 880L5252 880"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 839h0M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5252 880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lucca. <a href="/atlas?year=686&amp;focus=duchy-of-tuscia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
